  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 PM MDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nAfternoon satellite imagery shows virtually no cloud cover \nacross eastern Idaho as quiet and abnormally warm October \nweather continues. A stable synoptic regime will maintain this \npattern through the end of the workweek, with only subtle day- \nto-day temperature fluctuations and no appreciable chances for \nprecipitation. Highs will run well above seasonal climatological\nnorms, accompanied by light winds and clear overnight skies.\n\nWeather conditions begin to transition drastically on Saturday \nas a potent upper-level trough approaches the Intermountain \nWest. Surface winds will pick up noticeably during the day as \nthe regional pressure gradient tightens. Increased cloud cover \nand wind mixing will initiate a cooling trend, though daytime \nmaximum temperatures will still manage to hold a few degrees \nabove seasonal averages across most valleys. Cloud cover and \nprecipitation probabilities will increase rapidly during the \nsecond half of Saturday, spreading from southwest to northeast.\n\nA major airmass change overspreads eastern Idaho for the second\nhalf of the weekend into early next week, dropping temperatures\nwell below seasonal normals. Operational models and have trended\ntoward consolidating a cutoff low over central California and\nNevada Sunday into Monday but various ensembles still have\nslightly different opinions on where the low eventually goes.\nAssuming the former solutions pans out, this synoptic evolution\nwould lock a moist southerly to southwesterly flow over eastern\nIdaho, maintaining persistent precipitation chances through\nearly next week. While a cutoff low trajectory can result in\nslightly milder thermal profiles compared to a deep open trough,\nit significantly increases QPF potential and duration. High-\nelevation snowfall is looking increasingly likely, and depending\non how dynamically cold air is pulled into the low's\ncirculation, snow could potentially mix down to the valley\nfloors of the Snake Plain and Magic Valley. While minor\nuncertainty remains regarding the exact track and timing of the\nlow, confidence is very high in a prolonged transition to a\ncool, wet autumn pattern. Below-normal temperatures and periodic\nrain/snow showers will persist into next week. Forecast updates\nwill refine snow levels and QPF totals as guidance converges\nover the coming days.",
